The overlay is underneath because it is positioned relative to the page, not relative to the canvas.

This line does not do what you expect:

```html
<canvas ... position="relative"></canvas>
```

`position` is a CSS property, not a valid canvas HTML attribute. Also, the overlay needs a positioned parent with the same dimensions as the canvas.

## 1. Add a wrapper around the canvas

Replace this:

```html
<canvas id="gameCanvas" width="400"
        height="400" position="relative"></canvas>
<div id="overlay" style="position: absolute"></div>
```

with:

```html
<div id="gameContainer">
    <canvas id="gameCanvas" width="400" height="400"></canvas>
    <div id="overlay"></div>
</div>
```

The overlay is now inside the same container as the canvas.

## 2. Add the popup styling

Replace the current canvas CSS with:

```css
#gameContainer {
    position: relative;
    width: 400px;
    height: 400px;
}

#gameCanvas {
    display: block;
    border: 1px solid black;
}

#overlay {
    position: absolute;
    inset: 0;
    display: flex;
    flex-direction: column;
    justify-content: center;
    align-items: center;
    box-sizing: border-box;
    padding: 20px;
    color: white;
    background: rgba(0, 0, 0, 0.75);
    text-align: center;
    z-index: 1;
}
```

The important properties are:

- `position: relative` on `#gameContainer`: establishes the positioning reference.
- `position: absolute` on `#overlay`: places it over the container.
- `inset: 0`: makes it fill the entire 400 × 400 game area.
- `background: rgba(...)`: gives it a visible semi-transparent background.
- `z-index: 1`: puts it above the canvas.
- `display: flex`: centers the popup text.

Your canvas remains visible through the semi-transparent background, so it looks like a real overlay.

## 3. Remove the inline style

After adding the CSS, remove:

```html
